# mm.audio

A portable audio interface of three interface classes: In, a source of sampled
sound; Out, a sink for it; and Stream, the connection that carries samples from
one to the other, or between either one and an application. It knows nothing
about pins, transports, or boards. The concrete devices are a board's: its
platform provider derives a Microphone from In and a Speaker from Out over
whatever mm.mcu transport the board has -- paced ADC, DAC, or I2S -- and
registers one of each.

Sound is continuous and every converter keeps its own clock, so the Stream is
what absorbs the difference between the converter's clock and the program's.
It has one producer and one consumer, and every implementation is correct with
its two ends in two contexts. Ring is the implementation shipped here: a ring over
storage the caller owns, which never allocates, with two indices each written
by one side through plain atomic loads and stores.

A sample is a signed sixteen-bit integer and this version is mono, so every
count is in samples. Floating point is refused for mm.imu's reason. The sample
arithmetic converts to and from the widths transports carry, taking the width
as an argument because which width a transport has is the board's fact.

Nothing here calls back into the application and no interrupt touches a
Stream: a device's service moves samples between its Stream and a buffer the
platform owns, on the caller's thread. Rates are nominal in Format and exact in
Rate, because two devices asked for one rate generally run at two; correcting
the difference is policy above this module.

The platform-interface marker makes a board's audio provider part of an
executable only when its closure reaches mm.audio. mm.audio uses nothing but
the standard library.
